    publishedAt: "2026-11-26",
    excerpt: "Phishing messages imitate trusted people and organisations to steal information or money. Learn a safe pause-check-report routine.",
    sections: [
      { heading: "What phishing looks like today", html: `<p>Phishing is a form of social engineering. An attacker sends a message that tries to make you reveal information, open a harmful attachment, approve a login or send money. The message may arrive by email, SMS, messaging app, social media or phone.</p><p>Some scams contain spelling mistakes or strange formatting, but many are carefully written and visually convincing. Good grammar, a familiar logo or your name in the greeting does not prove that a message is genuine.</p>` },
      { heading: "Warning signs worth checking", html: `<ul><li><strong>Unexpected urgency:</strong> a threat of account closure, a payment deadline or a demand to act immediately.</li><li><strong>Requests for secrets:</strong> passwords, one-time codes, PINs, banking details or identity documents.</li><li><strong>Unexpected attachments:</strong> invoices, delivery notices or documents you were not expecting.</li><li><strong>Link mismatch:</strong> the visible wording suggests one organisation but the actual destination is different, shortened or misspelled.</li><li><strong>Unusual payment instructions:</strong> a changed bank account, gift-card request or pressure to keep a transaction secret.</li><li><strong>Unexpected contact:</strong> a message that claims to be from a colleague, supplier or family member but asks you to bypass normal checks.</li></ul><p>One warning sign is enough to pause. You do not need to prove a message is malicious before choosing a safer route.</p>` },
      { heading: "Check through a separate trusted route", html: `<p>Do not use the phone number, reply address or link supplied in the suspicious message. Open the organisation's official app, type its known website address yourself or call a number from a statement or official contact page. For a workplace request, confirm through your normal internal channel.</p><p>For links, inspect the full domain carefully. A familiar brand name appearing somewhere inside a long address does not mean the brand owns that domain. If you are unsure, do not open it.</p>` },
      { heading: "If you receive a suspicious message", html: `<ol><li>Do not reply, click, download, scan a QR code or approve a login request.</li><li>Report it using your email or messaging service's reporting option.</li><li>If it impersonates a bank, employer or service, contact that organisation using independently verified details.</li><li>Delete or quarantine the message after reporting, following your organisation's process if it is a work device.</li></ol><p>Never forward a suspicious attachment to colleagues as a warning. Share the reporting route or a safe screenshot with sensitive information removed.</p>` },
      { heading: "If you already clicked or shared information", html: `<p>Act quickly, but do not panic. If you entered a password, change it from the genuine service using a trusted device. Change it anywhere else you reused it, enable multi-factor authentication and review active sessions. If you shared banking information or sent money, contact your bank immediately through its official number. If you downloaded a file, disconnect the affected device from networks if you suspect malware and contact a trusted IT professional or your organisation's security team.</p><p>Report the incident honestly. Early reporting can limit damage and help protect other people. Do not delete evidence that your bank, employer or support team may need.</p>` },
      { heading: "Build a simple habit", html: `<p>Use a pause-check-report routine whenever a message creates pressure or asks you to take an unusual action. Keep software updated, use unique passwords stored in a reputable password manager and turn on multi-factor authentication. These measures do not make phishing impossible, but they reduce the chance that one mistake becomes an account takeover.</p><p>For workplace payment changes or requests for sensitive data, use a second-person approval process rather than relying on the message alone.</p>` },
