    slug: "clean-spreadsheet-data",
    title: "How to Clean Spreadsheet Data Before You Analyse It",
    description: "A practical guide to cleaning Excel or Google Sheets data: consistent headers, duplicates, blank rows, dates, text and quality checks.",
    courseSlug: "data-analysis-spreadsheets",
    category: "Data & Analytics",
    readTime: "8 min read",
    publishedAt: "2026-11-26",
    excerpt: "Reliable spreadsheet analysis starts with reliable data. Learn a repeatable cleaning process that protects the original file and makes errors easier to find.",
    sections: [
      { heading: "Why cleaning comes before formulas", html: `<p>A spreadsheet can look tidy and still contain problems that distort totals. The same customer may appear under two spellings, dates may be stored as text, blank rows may interrupt a range, and duplicate transactions may inflate revenue. If you analyse the file before checking its structure, a polished chart can make a wrong answer look convincing.</p><p>Data cleaning is not about making every cell look attractive. It is about making the records consistent enough to answer a specific question without hiding important exceptions.</p>` },
      { heading: "Protect the original data", html: `<ol><li>Keep an untouched copy of the original file.</li><li>Work on a duplicate or a clearly named cleaned-data sheet.</li><li>Record the changes you make, especially when removing or combining records.</li><li>Do not delete unusual values simply because they look inconvenient. Check what they represent first.</li></ol><p>This gives you a way to explain and reverse decisions if a total changes unexpectedly.</p>` },
      { heading: "Use one clear table structure", html: `<p>For a typical transaction dataset, use one row per transaction and one column per field. Keep a single header row with unique, non-empty names such as OrderDate, Branch, Product, Quantity and Amount. Avoid merged cells, decorative subtotal rows inside the data and multiple header rows.</p><p>Each column should contain one kind of information. Do not combine a customer's name and phone number in one cell if you need to analyse them separately. Keep dates as dates and quantities as numbers rather than mixing text labels into numeric columns.</p>` },
      { heading: "Check common data-quality problems", html: `<ul><li><strong>Blank rows and columns:</strong> identify whether they are genuinely empty or mark missing information.</li><li><strong>Duplicates:</strong> compare the fields that define a unique record. Two orders from the same customer are not duplicates just because the names match.</li><li><strong>Inconsistent text:</strong> standardise spelling, spacing and capitalisation where the values mean the same thing.</li><li><strong>Dates:</strong> check regional date formats and confirm that dates are recognised as dates, not text.</li><li><strong>Numbers:</strong> look for currency symbols, commas, spaces or text values that prevent calculations.</li><li><strong>Missing values:</strong> distinguish unknown, not applicable and genuinely zero. They do not mean the same thing.</li></ul>` },
      { heading: "Validate the cleaned result", html: `<p>Compare row counts before and after cleaning. Reconcile key totals such as transaction count and revenue. Filter each important column to inspect unexpected values. Check the earliest and latest dates, minimum and maximum quantities, and whether category totals add up to the overall total.</p><p>Keep a short cleaning log: what changed, why it changed and how you checked it. If a value was ambiguous, record the decision instead of silently guessing.</p>` },
      { heading: "Only then begin analysis", html: `<p>Once the structure is consistent, convert the range to a table where your spreadsheet tool supports it. Tables make it easier to filter, reference columns and extend formulas when new rows are added. Then define the question you want to answer before choosing formulas, summaries or charts.</p><p>For a guided workflow, practise on the deliberately messy café sales dataset in the <a href="/learning/courses/data-analysis-spreadsheets/">Boztik Data Analysis &amp; Spreadsheet Skills course</a>.</p>` },
