    excerpt: "A course certificate can support your CV when you explain what you learned and what you can demonstrate. Here's how to present it accurately.",
    sections: [
      { heading: "Use a clear section heading", html: `<p>If you have completed relevant short courses, add a section called “Professional Development”, “Additional Training” or “Relevant Courses”. Place it where it supports the role you are applying for. For an entry-level role, relevant learning may sit above unrelated work history; for an experienced applicant, it may fit below employment and qualifications.</p><p>Do not place an independently issued course certificate under a heading that implies a formal degree, licence or accredited qualification.</p>` },
      { heading: "Write a concise, factual entry", html: `<p>Include the course title, provider, completion month and year, and a verification link if one is available. Add one short line describing the relevant skills or practical work. Keep the wording factual and avoid claims that the course did not assess.</p><blockquote><p><strong>Example</strong><br>Practical Artificial Intelligence &amp; AI Productivity — Boztik Learning Hub, November 2026<br>Completed structured training in prompt design, AI-assisted research, output verification and responsible use. Certificate of Achievement: [verification URL].</p></blockquote><p>Replace the example date and skills with your actual completion details. Do not list a course as completed before you have passed its assessment.</p>` },
      { heading: "Describe evidence, not just attendance", html: `<p>Employers are more likely to understand a skill when you can explain how you used it. Where appropriate, link to a small portfolio item, practical exercise or project that you created yourself. For example, a spreadsheet course could lead to a cleaned sample dataset and a short report; an AI course could lead to a documented workflow with checks for accuracy and privacy.</p><p>Never include confidential workplace data, personal information or an employer's intellectual property in a public portfolio.</p>` },
      { heading: "Be transparent about accreditation", html: `<p>Not every online course is formally accredited. If a provider issues an independent certificate of completion or achievement, describe it that way. Do not call it a diploma, professional certification or accredited qualification unless the relevant awarding body and accreditation status genuinely support that wording.</p><p>Boztik courses are independently developed and not formally accredited. Boztik certificates record successful completion of the stated course assessment; they are not academic qualifications, professional licences or a guarantee of employment. This distinction should remain clear on your CV and LinkedIn profile.</p>` },
